---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const personSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  name: "Oryan Fuchs",
  jobTitle: "Software engineer",
  url: "https://gettoppicks.com/about",
  worksFor: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Top Picks",
    url: "https://gettoppicks.com/",
  },
};
---

<Base
  title="About Top Picks"
  description="Top Picks is written by Oryan Fuchs, a software engineer. How the hosting and email rankings at gettoppicks.com are researched, scored, and funded."
  path="/about"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(personSchema)} />
  <main class="wrap prose">
    <div class="kicker">About</div>
    <h1>Independent rankings, on purpose.</h1>
    <p>
      Top Picks is a small editorial site at gettoppicks.com. It is written by
      <strong>Oryan Fuchs</strong>, a software engineer. It exists to answer a
      narrow questions: which web host you should actually buy in 2026 if you are launching
      a site, a shop, or a side project — and which email tool you should attach once that
      site exists. Not enterprise platforms. Not a 40-logo roundup.
    </p>
    <h2>Who writes this</h2>
    <p>
      There is no newsroom and no lab. I research public pricing, product pages, and
      how these tools behave when you try to get a site live, then I write the ranking.
      When a claim depends on a promo, I say so. When I have not measured something —
      uptime, TTFB, support wait times — I do not invent a decimal for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The internet is full of hosting roundups that read like they were assembled from
      affiliate briefs. Twenty logos, identical scores, and a “winner” that happens to
      pay the highest commission. This site is the opposite shape: five providers, a
      written argument for each, and a method you can disagree with.
    </p>
    <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
    <p>
      Some outbound buttons are affiliate links. If you buy hosting or an email tool after clicking,
      the provider may pay Top Picks a commission. That should not change the price you
      see. It does create a conflict of interest, which is why the ranking method and
      the <a href="/disclosure">disclosure</a> are public.
    </p>
    <p>
      Order is not for sale. A program that is not approved yet still appears if it
      belongs on the list. A program with a higher payout does not automatically move up.
    </p>
    <h2>What will change</h2>
    <p>
      This is an early version of the rankings. Scores will move when pricing changes,
      when a product gets worse for the audience on its badge, or when real usage
      contradicts the write-up. They will not move because a vendor sent a pitch deck.
    </p>
    <p>
      If something on a review is wrong, the fix belongs in the article — not in a
      buried footnote. Start with the <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> if you
      want to know why Hostinger is first for most people and Hetzner is first for
      anyone who already has SSH keys.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
